sakura-windows-app follows the approach of Zorin Exec Guard:

    https://github.com/ZorinOS/zorin-exec-guard
    Copyright 2018-2022 Zorin OS Technologies Ltd.
    GPL-3.0

That program is not shipped here. It is GTK3, and its installation path is
written against apt and Zorin's own packages, neither of which exists on an
Arch-based system. The idea -- that double-clicking "Firefox Setup.exe" on
Linux is somebody trying to get Firefox rather than somebody trying to use
Wine, and that the right answer is to offer them Firefox -- is theirs.

Design note: one prefix per application
---------------------------------------
The separate-prefix model is Bottles' (usebottles.com, GPL-3.0), reached via
zorin-windows-app-support 2.0, which installs Bottles from Flathub alongside
Wine. Bottles itself is not shipped -- it is a GTK4/libadwaita Flatpak, and on
Plasma it looks like a guest -- and this is not a reimplementation of it. It
takes one idea: an application gets its own prefix, so removing it is deleting
a directory. Bottles does runner management, dependency installation and
sandboxing; this does not.
